\subsection{Differential Depth Response}
\label{sec:results-differential}

Features exhibit \emph{differential depth response} (DDR): central-tendency and diversity measures drift monotonically with depth, while shape measures remain near-stationary, consistent with recursive generation inducing a location shift in the NLL distribution while approximately preserving its functional form (shape-feature CV shift $<$3\% vs.\ $>$15\% for drifting features).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig1_differential_response.pdf}
\caption{Differential depth response: Spearman correlations between individual features and generation depth. Central-tendency and diversity features (hapax ratio, TTR, entropy mean) show substantial monotonic drift; entropy std ($|\rho|{=}0.17$) occupies an intermediate position; shape features (entropy max, tail decay) remain near-stationary ($|\rho| < 0.10$).}
\label{fig:feature-drift}
\end{figure}

Pooled class-balanced Spearman correlations with depth confirm the split (Figure~\ref{fig:feature-drift}):
drifting features show substantial monotonic trends (hapax ratio $\rho = -0.63$, TTR $\rho = -0.61$, entropy mean $\rho = -0.60$),
while shape features remain near-stationary (tail decay $|\rho| = 0.04$).
The six-fold magnitude gap between drifting ($|\rho| > 0.60$) and shape features ($|\rho| < 0.10$) serves as the operative criterion for the DDR split, with entropy std occupying an intermediate position ($|\rho| = 0.17$); shifting these descriptive thresholds by $\pm 0.10$ does not alter feature grouping.

\paragraph{Distributional shift quantification.}
Two-sample KS tests between gen-1 and gen-2+ distributions confirm the split: drifting-feature KS statistics (0.516--0.537) are ${\sim}10\times$ larger than shape-feature statistics (0.043--0.052; all $p \approx 0$ after Bonferroni correction).
Levene tests reject equal variance for all features ($p < 10^{-10}$), but effect sizes differ markedly: shape-feature CV shift $<$3\% vs.\ $>$15\% for drifting features, which supports ``approximately preserves'' as a practical characterization rather than a strict invariance claim.

\paragraph{Feature complementarity.}
Under 10-fold stratified CV, the full 7-feature set (F1\,=\,0.773) outperforms surface-only (F1\,=\,0.688) and reference-only (F1\,=\,0.713; McNemar $p \approx 0$; Table~\ref{tab:ablation} in Appendix~\ref{app:ablation-table}).
Reference-only yields higher binary F1 (0.833 vs.\ 0.695) since surface features introduce noise at the gen-1/gen-2+ boundary, but surface features are essential for 3-class gen-0 discrimination (Full 0.773 vs.\ Reference-only 0.713).
Permutation importance: hapax ratio (0.35), entropy mean (0.23), TTR (0.18); leave-one-feature-out confirms 6 of 7 features contribute positively (Appendix~\ref{app:lofo}).

\paragraph{Cross-family reference models.}
Alternative references (OLMo-2-1B, Qwen3-0.6B) achieve comparable LOGO F1 (spread 0.013; Table~\ref{tab:cross-ref} in Appendix~\ref{app:cross-ref}), with consistently negative entropy drift, confirming DDR is a property of recursive generation.

\paragraph{Temperature sensitivity.}
The method operates reliably within $t \in [0.5, 0.9]$: F1 varies by at most 1\,pp from the default $t{=}0.7$ across this range (Appendix~\ref{app:sampling-sensitivity}).
At $t{=}1.0$, performance degrades by ${\sim}6$\,pp as the primary depth signal collapses: entropy mean's Spearman correlation with depth drops from $\rho = -0.60$ to $\rho = -0.002$, indicating that high-temperature sampling injects sufficient token-level randomness to mask the systematic location shift that DDR exploits.


\subsection{Comparison with Detection Baselines}
\label{sec:results-baselines}

We repurpose four detection scores as ordinal depth predictors using the same XGBoost framework and LOGO splits (Table~\ref{tab:baselines}, Figure~\ref{fig:baseline-ci}).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig3_baseline_comparison.pdf}
\caption{LOGO binary F1 with 95\% bootstrap CIs. Per-generator variance is higher for single detectors than for the ensemble.}
\label{fig:baseline-ci}
\end{figure}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\footnotesize
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Method} & \textbf{Feat.} & \textbf{3-cl F1} & \textbf{Bin F1} & \textbf{ms/text} \\
\midrule
4-det.\ + surface     & 6 & \textbf{0.795} & \textbf{0.708} & 22.8 \\
4-detector ens.\      & 4 & 0.785 & 0.700 & 22.8 \\
7-feature (ours)      & 7 & 0.774 & 0.705 & \textbf{9.7} \\
Avg-LogProb           & 1 & 0.707 & 0.673 & --- \\
FastDetectGPT         & 1 & 0.660 & 0.569 & --- \\
LD-Score              & 1 & 0.630 & 0.675 & --- \\
Binoculars            & 1 & 0.411 & 0.539 & --- \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Depth classification (8-gen LOGO, pooled evaluation: predictions from all held-out folds are concatenated before computing F1, yielding slightly higher scores than the per-generator macro average in Table~\ref{tab:logo-results}). All methods use identical XGBoost configuration ($n_\text{estimators}{=}200$, $\max_\text{depth}{=}6$). Binary: gen-1 vs.\ gen-2+. Inference cost: 7-feature requires one 410M reference model (905\,MB); 4-det ensembles require two models totaling 1.4B parameters (2{,}868\,MB).}
\label{tab:baselines}
\end{table}

The 7-feature ensemble exceeds all individual baselines in 3-class F1 ($\Delta$\,=\,6.7--14.4\,pp vs.\ Avg-LogProb through LD-Score).
Multi-detector ensembles achieve higher 3-class F1 (0.795 vs.\ 0.774, +2.1\,pp) and marginally higher binary F1 (0.708 vs.\ 0.705, +0.3\,pp), but require 2.4$\times$ longer inference and 3.2$\times$ more GPU memory.


Preliminary downstream evaluation (fine-tuning OLMo-2-0425-1B on depth-stratified data; Appendix~\ref{app:downstream}) finds that depth composition does not determine training utility at fixed sample count, though the evidence is limited (three seeds, single perplexity metric).

We evaluate depth fingerprinting under three prompt types using Pythia-2.8B: \emph{continuation} (``Continue the following text''), \emph{elaboration} (``Elaborate on the following text''), and \emph{inspired rewriting} (``Write a new passage inspired by the following text''). Each uses 995 seeds $\times$ 5 depths.

\paragraph{Within-prompt-type classification.}
5-fold CV macro-F1: continuation 0.78, rewriting 0.74, elaboration 0.73. All three prompt types exhibit the same differential depth response pattern: drifting features (hapax ratio, TTR, entropy mean) show consistent monotonic trends, while stationary features (entropy std, tail decay) change by less than 5\%.

\paragraph{Cross-prompt-type transfer (LOPTO).}
Leave-one-prompt-type-out evaluation yields average F1\,=\,0.753, confirming that depth signal transfers across prompt types. Feature decay rates differ: continuation shows the strongest drift (16.9\% per depth step), followed by rewriting (6.1\%) and elaboration (1.4\%), consistent with continuation chains applying less semantic constraint on the output.

To assess whether depth signal extends beyond handcrafted features, we fine-tune a pretrained language model end-to-end as a depth classifier using QLoRA \cite{qlora}.

\paragraph{Setup.}
We fine-tune Qwen3-0.6B-Base with LoRA rank 16, learning rate $2 \times 10^{-4}$, and 3 epochs, framing depth estimation as 5-class sequence classification (gen-0 through gen-4).\footnote{Evaluated on the original 6-generator subset; XGBoost 3-class F1\,=\,0.789. We expect 8-generator training to further improve given additional diversity; the comparison direction is preserved.}
For 3-class evaluation, we collapse predictions into gen-0\,/\,gen-1\,/\,gen-2+ post hoc.
All results use the same LOGO protocol as the main experiments.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\caption{QLoRA end-to-end depth classification vs.\ XGBoost on handcrafted features, under LOGO evaluation.}
\label{tab:qlora}
\resizebox{\linewidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Method} & \textbf{5-class F1} & \textbf{3-class F1} & \textbf{Per-class F1 (5-class)} \\
\midrule
XGBoost (7 features) & 0.501 $\pm$ 0.038 & 0.789 & --- \\
QLoRA (Qwen3-0.6B)   & 0.583 $\pm$ 0.010 & 0.905 $\pm$ 0.003 & 0.999\,/\,0.796\,/\,0.413\,/\,0.259\,/\,0.447 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Results.}
QLoRA achieves 3-class LOGO F1\,=\,0.905 ($\pm$0.003), a +11.6\,pp improvement over the XGBoost baseline (0.789).
On 5-class evaluation, QLoRA reaches F1\,=\,0.583 ($\pm$0.010) vs.\ 0.501 for XGBoost (+8.2\,pp), with the largest gains at gen-1 (0.796 vs.\ 0.634) and gen-2 (0.413 vs.\ 0.268).
The gen-2/gen-3 boundary remains the primary failure mode (gen-3 F1\,=\,0.259), consistent with the feature-level analysis in \S\ref{sec:discussion}.

\paragraph{Hyperparameter robustness.}
A second run with LoRA rank 32 and seed 123 yields 5-class F1\,=\,0.587 ($\pm$0.005) and 3-class F1\,=\,0.905 ($\pm$0.004), confirming stability across hyperparameter and seed variation ($\Delta < 1$\,pp on both metrics).

\paragraph{Implications.}
The substantial 3-class improvement demonstrates that raw token embeddings contain depth signal beyond what summary statistics capture; the seven handcrafted features are a strong but lossy compression of the full distributional information.
End-to-end fine-tuning exploits token-level patterns (e.g., positional regularities, n-gram repetitions) that aggregate features cannot represent.
This confirms that the depth signal documented in our main analysis is real and exploitable beyond engineered features, while the handcrafted approach retains practical advantages: interpretability, computational efficiency (no GPU inference), and the ability to reveal the differential depth response pattern that motivates the task formulation.

Figure~\ref{fig:cross-temp} shows cross-temperature transfer results for the 6-generator LOGO pipeline.
We evaluate four train$\rightarrow$test temperature conditions using Qwen3-1.7B data regenerated at $t{=}1.0$ (remaining five generators retain $t{=}0.7$).

\begin{figure*}[ht]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig_appendix_cross_temp.pdf}
\caption{Cross-temperature transfer evaluation (6-gen LOGO). (a) Overall F1 by train$\rightarrow$test temperature condition. Matched conditions (0.7$\rightarrow$0.7, 1.0$\rightarrow$1.0) outperform cross-temperature transfer, with 0.7$\rightarrow$1.0 showing the largest degradation ($-6.6$\,pp in 3-class F1). (b) Per-generator 3-class F1 heatmap: Qwen3-1.7B shows dramatic degradation under cross-temperature conditions (0.418) while other generators remain stable, indicating the temperature mismatch disproportionately affects the generator whose data was regenerated.}
\label{fig:cross-temp}
\end{figure*}

Key observations from the cross-temperature analysis:

\begin{enumerate}[nosep]
\item \textbf{Matched-temperature robustness}: Both $t{=}0.7$ and $t{=}1.0$ matched conditions yield comparable performance (3-class F1: 0.789 vs.\ 0.726), with the gap attributable to weaker depth signal at high temperature rather than classifier degradation.

\item \textbf{Asymmetric transfer}: Training at $t{=}1.0$ and testing at $t{=}0.7$ (F1\,=\,0.791) transfers well, but the reverse (0.7$\rightarrow$1.0, F1\,=\,0.723) degrades. This asymmetry reflects the information structure: features learned from high-variance (high-temperature) data generalize to low-variance settings, but not vice versa.

\item \textbf{Generator-specific vulnerability}: Qwen3-1.7B, the only generator with temperature-mismatched test data, drops to F1\,=\,0.418 under cross-temperature conditions while all other generators maintain F1 $>$ 0.76. This confirms that depth features are calibrated to the specific decoding regime.

\item \textbf{Binary robustness}: Binary classification (gen-0/1 vs.\ gen-2+) is more robust to temperature mismatch (0.900--0.958 across all conditions) than 3-class, suggesting the coarser gen-0 vs.\ machine boundary is less sensitive to decoding parameters than fine-grained depth discrimination.
\end{enumerate}

